Project Management Plan – Simple Study Notes
1. What is a Project Management Plan?
According to PMBOK 7:

A Project Management Plan is a document that describes how a project will be executed, monitored, controlled and closed.

In simple words:
- It is a roadmap for managing a project.
- It combines subsidiary management plans, baselines and other important information.
- It explains how the project team will achieve project objectives.
- It is used as a reference throughout the project.
- It helps new team members understand the project.
Main purpose: To explain how all project work should be performed successfully.
Difference between Project Charter and Project Management Plan
Project Charter	Project Management Plan
Formally starts the project.	Explains how to manage the project.
Gives high-level information.	Gives detailed information.
Explains what and why.	Explains how, when and who.
Authorizes the project manager.	Guides the project manager and team.

Importance of Project Management Planning
1. Provides a starting point for the project.
2. Makes the project more organized.
3. Clearly defines the project scope.
4. Helps manage the project efficiently.
5. Builds confidence among stakeholders.

2. Main Components of a Project Management Plan
The Project Management Plan includes three main areas.
A. Subsidiary Management Plans
These are smaller plans that explain how different project areas are managed.

Examples:
1. Scope Management Plan
2. Schedule Management Plan
3. Cost Management Plan
4. Quality Management Plan
5. Human Resource Plan
6. Communications Management Plan
7. Risk Management Plan
8. Procurement Management Plan
B. Baselines
Baselines are approved plans used to compare actual project performance with planned performance.

There are three main baselines:

1. Scope Baseline
- Includes Scope Statement, WBS and WBS Dictionary.
- Defines the approved project scope.
2. Schedule Baseline
- Approved version of the project schedule.
- Used to compare planned time with actual time.
3. Cost Baseline
- Approved time-based project budget.
- Used to compare planned costs with actual costs.
C. Other Information
Other information may include:
- Change Management Plan – explains how project changes are handled.
- Development Approach – explains how the product or service will be developed.
- Project Life Cycle – explains the project phases.

3. Content of a Project Management Plan
The contents can change depending on the size and complexity of the project.

There are 12 important components.
1. Scope Statement
- Explains what work should be completed in the project.
- Defines project requirements and boundaries.
- Helps stakeholders understand and agree on the project scope.
The scope statement should follow SMART:
- S – Specific
- M – Measurable
- A – Achievable
- R – Relevant
- T – Time-bound
2. Critical Success Factors (CSF)
- Important factors that must be achieved for project success.
- Every team member should understand them.
Example – Bridge construction:
- Quality construction materials.
- Skilled workers.
- Enough budget.
- Proper time management.
3. Deliverables
- Deliverables are the products, services or results produced by a project.
- They must meet the project requirements.
Examples:
- Construction of a new road.
- Building a 6-foot boundary wall.
- Developing a software system.
4. Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)
- Divides a large project into smaller tasks.
- Makes project work easier to manage.
- Helps assign tasks to team members.
- Helps identify the work needed to complete deliverables.
5. Schedule
- Shows when each project activity starts and ends.
- Estimates the time needed for each task.
- Shows task order and dependencies.
- Helps complete the project on time.
Example – Boundary wall construction:
Task	Activity	Start	End
1	Setting out	Aug 1	Aug 2
2	Excavation	Aug 2	Aug 4
3	Foundation	Aug 4	Aug 6
4	Masonry work	Aug 6	Aug 10
5	Finishing work	Aug 10	Aug 12

6. Budget
- Shows the estimated total cost of the project.
- Includes payments for workers, materials and services.
- Helps control project spending.
7. Quality
- Defines the quality standards for the project.
- Explains how the team will maintain and check quality.
- Ensures the final product meets customer requirements.
8. Human Resource Plan
- Explains who will work on the project.
- Defines team members' roles and responsibilities.
- Explains how project staff are selected and managed.
Main elements:
- Resource requirements.
- Project team acquisition.
- Training and development.
- Team management.
9. Stakeholder List
- Lists people who are interested in or affected by the project.
- Includes internal and external stakeholders.
- Helps understand their needs and expectations.
Examples: Project manager, team members, customers, suppliers and sponsors.
10. Communication Plan
- Explains how project information is shared.
- Identifies who sends and receives information.
- Defines when, how and what information is communicated.
- Helps everyone stay informed about project progress.
11. Risk Register
- A document that lists important project risks.
- Helps the team prepare for possible problems.
- Includes possible risks and how to respond to them.
Main details in a risk register:
- Risk description.
- Likelihood (chance of happening).
- Impact (effect on the project).
- Priority.
- Triggers (warning signs).
- Response plan.
12. Procurement Plan
- Explains what products or services must be purchased from outside the project team.
- Identifies possible suppliers.
- Explains what to buy, how much to buy and when to buy.

4. Develop Project Management Plan
Meaning: This process creates a document that explains how the project will be executed, monitored, controlled and closed.

It is a living document because it can be updated when project conditions change.
Inputs
Inputs are the information needed to develop the Project Management Plan.

There are four main inputs according to PMBOK 6:
1. Project Charter – formally authorizes the project and gives authority to the project manager.
2. Outputs from Other Processes – subsidiary plans and baselines created during other planning processes.
3. Enterprise Environmental Factors (EEF) – external and internal conditions that affect the project, such as laws and industry standards.
4. Organizational Process Assets (OPA) – organizational policies, processes, procedures and templates.
Tools and Techniques
These are the methods used to develop the plan.

1. Expert Judgment

- Getting advice from experienced people.

2. Data Gathering
- Brainstorming.
- Checklists.
- Focus groups.
- Interviews.
3. Interpersonal and Team Skills
- Conflict management.
- Facilitation.
- Meeting management.
4. Meetings

- Discussing and agreeing on the project plan with relevant people.
Output
Project Management Plan

The main output of this process is the completed Project Management Plan.
Develop Project Management Plan – Summary
Inputs

Project Charter + Outputs from Other Processes + EEF + OPA


Tools & Techniques

Expert Judgment + Data Gathering + Team Skills + Meetings


Output

Project Management Plan



5. Dynamic Use of the Project Management Plan
The Project Management Plan is a living document. It can be updated when project requirements or conditions change.

There are two situations:

Before baselines are approved:
- The plan can be updated as necessary.
- No formal change control process is required.
After baselines are approved:
- Changes must follow the formal change control process.
- Change requests must be submitted and approved.
- The Perform Integrated Change Control process is used.
This ensures that project changes are properly controlled until project closure.

